            <ArticleTitle>Adolescents' psychosocial experiences living with asthma: a focus group study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>99-107</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Asthma is influenced by and influences psychosocial experiences in adolescents. Therefore, the purpose of this study was to explore psychosocial experiences and coping strategies of a sample of adolescents with asthma based on their own accounts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Six focus group interviews were conducted in Central Virginia with 19 adolescents with asthma ages 12 to 18 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Three general descriptions of experiences with asthma were found in the data: &quot;Doing less with more effort,&quot; &quot;Missing out,&quot; and &quot;Not all bad.&quot; Adolescents' negative emotions were compounded by perceptions of others' responses. Three coping strategies, &quot;Toughening,&quot; &quot;Guardedness,&quot; and &quot;Modifying&quot; also were identified.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Findings enhance understanding of particular patterns of psychosocial experiences in adolescents and lay a foundation for developmentally appropriate asthma interventions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
